<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belvedere, Vienna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1714">1714</a>–<a href="/years/1723">1723</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Belvedere Palace</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Belvedere is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> palace complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-lukas-von-hildebrandt">Johann Lukas von Hildebrandt</a>, with the Lower Belvedere completed in 1716 and the Upper Belvedere in 1723. Its two palaces, linked by terraced gardens, are among the masterpieces of Viennese Baroque. Acquired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> after Eugene's death, it now houses the Austrian Gallery, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-klimt">Gustav Klimt</a>'s The Kiss.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belvedere-vienna.jpg" alt="Belvedere, Vienna" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palacio_Belvedere,_Viena,_Austria,_2020-02-01,_DD_93-95_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
